>>> Or to put it as simply as I can, a web browser can access all
>>> protocols (nntp,http,ftp etc) on the internet by itself.

No. A web browser can access a Usenet news service
provider site which allows news articles to be
written and read, but NNTP is a background protocol,
used by those sites to send Usenet news articles
back and forth between themselves, out of sight of
the users. Similarly for email, though SMTP is used
in the background of email services, sometimes even
from the user's own computer, it isn't a foreground
service the user can call up as a hyperlink.

FTP, on the otherhand, is exactly such a service,
there is a URI prefix "ftp:", ftp is so well
integrated into most browsers the user doesn't need
to notice that the data being accessed is being
transported by FTP instead of by HTTP. Other
integrated protocols are picked and chosen on a
browser by browser basis, I guess; SeaMonkey (nee
"Mozilla") in its current incarnation accepts
"gopher:" but not "wais:", suggesting that gopher
may still be in use while wais is really defunct.

>> As far as I understand the issue a browser that
>> accepts, e.g., an "ftp:" URI must implement the
>> ftp protocol (or delegate it to another ftp
>> client). What the browser then does is just
>> displaying the files and directories.

Since "FTP" is just a transport protocol, and a
pretty simple minded one (until you get down to all
the commands it supports when used from the command
line), that constitutes a fairly complete "read
only" implementation. The commands are built and
exchanged between the browser and the FTP server,
the error messages that occur are displayed by the
browser, pretty much everything you need to access
the data fed by the FTP server is there.

If you want finder grain access, though, or write
access, you need to abandon doing FTP via stateless
browser means, and use a service where you can do an
FTP login to the target server, so bring up an
ssh/telnet service and do FTP from there.

